Q: How can I use the reduce/reject/map function for Laravel collections? A: <p>you don't need to make collect again $users,try like this</p>
<pre><code>$names = $users-&gt;reject(function ($user) {
                    return $user-&gt;active === false;
                 })-&gt;map(function ($user) {
                    return $user-&gt;name;
                 });
</code></pre>

Q: sum column value using datatable A: <p>You can try the following code:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;fnFooterCallback&quot;: function ( nRow, aaData, iStart, iEnd, aiDisplay ) {
         var api = this.api, aaData;
        /*
         * Calculate the total market share for all browsers in this table (ie inc. outside
         * the pagination)
         */
        var iTotalMarketColumns = api.column(11, {
            search: 'applied'
        }).cache('search');
        var iTotalMarket = iTotalMarketColumns.length ? iTotalMarketColumns.reduce(function(a, b) {
            return parseFloat(a) + parseFloat(b);
        }) : 0;


        /* Calculate the market share for browsers on this page */
        var iPageMarketColumns = api.column(11, {
            search: 'current'
        }).cache('search');
        var iPageMarket = iPageMarketColumns.length ? iPageMarketColumns.reduce(function(a, b) {
                return parseFloat(a) + parseFloat(b);
            }) : 0;

        /* Modify the footer row to match what we want */
        $(nRow).find('th').eq(1).text(parseInt(iPageMarket * 100)/100 +'% ('+ parseInt(iTotalMarket * 100)/100 +'% total)');
}
</code></pre>

Q: Decision Making Based On User Input A: <p>The author of this issue possibly has moved on from this, but the issue and good comments seemed to warrant an example of how string functionality could be used for testing and execution of calculation blocks in lieu of entering a single character and using character tests.  Following is a refactored version of the code prompting a user for the word &quot;Salary&quot; or &quot;Private&quot; and then calculating taxes based upon the entered type of income along with the income amount.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;

#define SIZE 40

int main(void)
{
    char c[SIZE];                                       /* Don't have CS50 installed - using customary variable definitions */
    int inc, tax;                                       /* Give variables meaningful names                                  */

    while (true)
    {
        printf(&quot;Income type (Salary/Private).: &quot;);

        if (fgets(c, SIZE - 1, stdin) == NULL)          /* Don't have CS50 installed - using customary input functions      */
            return 1;

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; strlen(c); i++)             /* Convert input to lowercase characters                            */
            c[i] = tolower(c[i]);

        c[strlen(c) - 1] = '\0';                        /* Scrub out any newline character(s) from fgets()                  */

        if (strcmp(c, &quot;salary&quot;) == 0 || strcmp(c, &quot;private&quot;) == 0)
            break;

        printf(&quot;Invalid entry - please retry\n&quot;);
    }

    printf(&quot;Your Income.........: &quot;);

    if (scanf(&quot;%d&quot;, &amp;inc) != 1)                         /* Don't have CS50 installed - using customary input functions      */
        return 2;

    if (strcmp(c, &quot;salary&quot;) == 0)                       /* Using strcmp() in lieu of character equality test                */
    {
        if (inc &lt;= 14800)
        {
            tax = inc * 0.15;
        }
        else if (inc &gt; 14800 &amp;&amp; inc &lt;= 34000)
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.15) + (inc - 14800) * 0.20;
        }
        else if (inc &gt; 34000 &amp;&amp; inc &lt;= 120000)
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.15) + ((34000 - 14800) * 0.20) + (inc - 34000) * 0.27;
        }
        else
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.15) + ( (34000 - 14800) * 0.20) + ((120000 - 34000) * 0.27) + (inc - 120000) * 0.35;
        }
    }
    else
    {
        if (inc &lt;= 14800)                               /* Employ different tax rates to illustrate differences             */
        {
            tax = inc * 0.14;
        }
        else if (inc &gt; 14800 &amp;&amp; inc &lt;= 34000)
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.14) + (inc - 14800) * 0.19;
        }
        else if (inc &gt; 34000 &amp;&amp; inc &lt;= 80000)
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.14) + ( (34000 - 14800) * 0.19) + (inc - 34000) * 0.26;
        }
        else
        {
            tax = (14800 * 0.14) + ( (34000 - 14800) * 0.19) + ((80000 - 34000) * 0.26) + (inc - 80000) * 0.34;
        }
    }
    printf(&quot;Your Income Tax.....: %d.00\n&quot;, tax);   /* Simplify code - only need one printf statement at the end    */
}
</code></pre>
<p>Key points to note here are as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>A character array variable (string) is used to accept input in lieu of a character.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To simplify validation of the entered selection, the characters are converted to all lowercase characters before determining that a valid input entry has been made.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The &quot;strcmp()&quot; function is utilized to determine if salary tax calculations should be used or private income tax calculations should be used.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To again streamline the code, the repetitive print statements are removed out of each &quot;bracket&quot; calculation and just one print statement is used at the end after the appropriate tax value has been calculated.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Also, as a side note, I did not have the CS50 library installed on my virtual machine, so the definitions and functions germane to the CS50 library were replaced with usual and customary C definitions and functions.  These substitutions do not altar how the program functions.</p>
<p>With that, following are a couple of tests of the refactored code with their terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Taxes/bin/Release$ ./Taxes 
Income type (Salary/Private).: Salary
Your Income.........: 20000
Your Income Tax.....: 3260.00
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Taxes/bin/Release$ ./Taxes 
Income type (Salary/Private).: Private
Your Income.........: 20000
Your Income Tax.....: 3060.00
</code></pre>
<p>The takeaway here would be to delve into C tutorials as they pertain to character and strings and the types of functions and testing that can be done on such variables.</p>
